; `AX3032` for a macro that IS in a module (2026-08-16) - the half of
; the message that did not exist while the entry file was the only
; way to reach the refusal.
;
; `useDup`'s template names `dup`, which `AmbMac` imports from
; `AmbB`. Importing `AmbA` here makes that name resolve to no single
; module, so qualification leaves it bare, and the `let` then binds
; it: the capture MAC-HYG-8 refuses rather than performs.
;
; Two things the entry-file case cannot pin:
;
;   the advice   "move the macro into a module" is no advice at all
;                for a macro already in one, so the message branches
;                on whether the macro has a module and this file is
;                the branch `590-macro-capture.ax` does not take
;   the span     the diagnostic is at the INVOCATION, in this file,
;                and the frame is at the macro. It used to stamp the
;                invocation's offset with the MACRO's unit, which
;                resolved against the wrong file - harmless while
;                both were the entry file, and off the end of an
;                8-line module the moment one was not
(import AmbMac)

(import AmbA)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let ((dup 
    (lambda (y) 0)
  ))
    (useDup 4)
  )
)
